 /*
  * Transport stream demultiplexer found on HiSilicon set-top box SoCs.
  *
- * This stage brings the input side up: the block is taken out of reset,
- * its clocks are started, and one of the four input ports is told what
- * shape of transport stream to expect.  The hardware then counts the
- * packets it receives on each port, which is reported through debugfs
- * and is enough to tell whether a tuned front end is actually feeding
- * the demultiplexer.
+ * The block is taken out of reset, its clocks are started, and one of
+ * the four input ports is told what shape of transport stream to
+ * expect.  From there the whole multiplex is recorded into a ring of
+ * blocks by direct memory access and handed to the DVB core, which
+ * filters it in software.
  *
- * Filtering and delivery to the DVB stack are not here yet.
+ * The hardware can filter by PID and even assemble sections itself,
+ * but the recording path delivers packets that are already aligned
+ * and never straddle a block, so software filtering is both simple
+ * and correct.  Hardware filtering can be added underneath later.
